<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hephthalites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hephthalite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hephthalites were a people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> who built a powerful state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and neighbouring regions in the fifth and sixth centuries CE. They threatened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>'s north-eastern frontier. Around 557 CE an alliance of the Sasanians and the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a> destroyed their empire, and the Gokturks expanded into former Hephthalite lands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Hephthalites, sometimes called the White Huns, called themselves Ebodalo, a name they wrote in the Bactrian script on some of their coins. Sources for their history are sparse, there is no king-list, and historians disagree about how the group arose and what language it first spoke. The most prominent view at present is that they were of Turkic origin and later adopted Bactrian, an Eastern Iranian language written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-alphabet">Greek alphabet</a>, as their official language. Chinese chronicles derived them from the Gaoju or the Da Yuezhi. Scholars have reached no consensus on any link with the Huns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Hephthalites were at first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a> and borrowed much of their political organisation from it, including the title khan. They split from their overlords in the early fifth century. Persian sources show them fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-ii">Yazdegerd II</a> from 442. In 458 a Hephthalite king named Akhshunwar helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a> seize the Sasanian throne from his brother, and with Hephthalite aid Peroz reportedly ended Kidarite rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> by 467.</p><p>Their stronghold was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a>, on the northern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>, and their capital was probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a>. They may have conquered Sogdia as early as 479. In the late fifth century they crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamir Mountains</a> and took the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and Khotan, from the weakening Rourans, and by the early sixth century they were sending embassies to the Northern Wei. They did not go beyond the Hindu Kush, which the Alchon Huns held, a people once thought to be part of the Hephthalites and now usually treated as separate.</p><p>Their power ended when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">First Turkic Khaganate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> allied against them and defeated them after an eight-day battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarshi">Qarshi</a>, the Battle of Gol-Zarriun, perhaps in 557. Afterwards Hephthalite principalities remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a> until the Tokhara Yabghus took over in 625.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Hephthalites were a tribal confederation that included both nomadic and settled urban communities, and many of the people they ruled were probably Iranian speakers. Central control was weak, and local dynasties paid tribute. The Chinese considered them excellent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, and they probably had a strong cavalry. Chinese sources report that brothers shared one wife, with the number of horns on a married woman's headdress matching the number of her husbands. Murals at Balalyk tepe and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan">Buddhas of Bamiyan</a> show figures with round beardless faces and cropped hair. The complex at Bamiyan was developed under Hephthalite rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hephthalites” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under his son Muqan Qaghan (553–572) the state expanded greatly: the remaining Rouran were crushed, the Khitans defeated in the east, and Bumin's brother Istämi, ruling the western wing, destroyed the <b>Hephthalites</b> in alliance with Sasanian Persia and pushed towards the Caspian.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Polyandry was the <b>Hephthalites</b>' most noted custom: brothers shared one wife, whose children belonged to the eldest, and the number of horns on a married woman's headdress showed how many husbands she had.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west Rouran influence reached Turfan, the Tarim oases and possibly the <b>Hephthalites</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 5th century the cities acknowledged in turn the overlordship of the <b>Hephthalites</b>, the Western Turks and, nominally, from the 650s, the Tang Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The influence of the art of Gandhara in some of the earliest, of around 500 CE, is put down to the political union of Bactria and Kucha under the <b>Hephthalites</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
